Q: What is the &quot;idiomatic Zig way&quot; of doing these matrix operations as functions? A: <p>Since matrices are generally used in high performance code, I would expect the Matrix type to statically allocate it's data. That way, arrays of matrix data can be created with excellent cache coherency (the memory all sits together). By using an allocator, you are potentially placing the matrix structure at a different place in memory than it's underlying data.</p>
<p>In addition, when you make the rows and cols part of the type, they don't have to exist in memory and they don't have to be checked in many cases.</p>
<p>So, something like:</p>
<pre><code>fn Matrix(T: type, comptime rows: usize, comptime cols: usize) type {
    return struct {
        data: [rows*cols]T,
    }
}
</code></pre>

